    <section id="global-overview">
        <h2>Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>
        <p>Over the past three decades, the global trajectory of intentional homicide has shown significant fluctuations, albeit with a relatively stable central tendency. The global median homicide rate stands at <strong>{global_median:.2f} per 100,000 population</strong> in the most recent available reporting years, indicating that for the majority of countries, homicide rates are comparatively low.</p>

        <div class="chart-container">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['global_trend']}" alt="Global Trend Chart">
        </div>

        <div class="insights">
            <strong>Key Insight: Mean vs. Median</strong><br>
            The substantial gap between the global mean and median indicates a heavily right-skewed distribution. A small number of countries experience exceptionally high homicide rates, pulling the mean up, while the typical (median) country remains much safer.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="regional-comparison">
        <h2>Regional & Country-Level Comparison</h2>
        <p>Disparities at the national level are stark. While many nations, particularly in Western Europe and parts of Asia, report homicide rates near zero, certain regions continue to face crisis-level violence.</p>

        <div class="chart-container">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['top_bottom']}" alt="Top and Bottom Countries Chart">
        </div>

        <div class="insights">
            <strong>Key Insight: The Extremes</strong><br>
            The highest recorded national rate in recent data belongs to <strong>{highest_country['country']}</strong> at <strong>{highest_country['homicide_rate']:.1f} per 100k</strong>. Conversely, the safest reporting countries consistently record rates below 0.5 per 100k, showcasing a vast inequality in global public safety.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="sex-disparity">
