        <div class="chart-container">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['gender_trend']}" alt="Gender Trend Chart">
        </div>

        <div class="insights">
            <strong>Key Insight: Persistent Inequality</strong><br>
            This male-to-female ratio has remained largely consistent over the observed 30-year period. While female homicide rates remain lower on average, they often correlate closely with domestic violence and intimate partner violence, whereas male rates are more closely tied to organized crime, gang violence, and broader societal conflict.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="age-group-analysis">
        <h2>Age Group Analysis</h2>
        <p>Age is a critical risk factor for homicide victimization. Our analysis breaks down the available data by specific age cohorts to identify the most vulnerable demographics.</p>

        <div class="chart-container">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['age_group']}" alt="Age Group Chart">
        </div>

        <div class="insights">
            <strong>Key Insight: The Youth Burden</strong><br>
            Young adults, particularly those in the 15-29 and 30-44 brackets, typically face the highest risk of homicide. This aligns with global trends where youth populations in heavily impacted regions are disproportionately involved in or victimized by violent crime.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="notable-trends">
        <h2>Notable Country-Level Trends & Outliers</h2>
        <p>Looking longitudinally across the dataset reveals nations that have experienced dramatic shifts in their public safety landscape—both success stories of massive crime reduction and tragic deteriorations.</p>

        <div class="chart-container">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['outliers']}" alt="Notable Trends Chart">
        </div>

        <div class="insights">
            <strong>Key Insight: Volatility</strong><br>
            The highlighted countries display the most extreme variance over the 1990-2023 period. Sharp increases often correspond to political instability, the rise of organized crime syndicates, or civil conflict, whereas sharp declines point toward successful state interventions, pacification policies, or the end of internal wars.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="data-limitations-recommendations">
        <h2>Data Limitations & Policy Recommendations</h2>
        <p>It is vital for policy analysts to understand the contours and limitations of the UNODC dataset when crafting recommendations.</p>

        <div class="chart-container">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['coverage']}" alt="Coverage Chart">
        </div>

