            <strong>Data Caveats:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Unbalanced Panel:</strong> Reporting coverage peaks around 80% but frequently drops lower. Trends must be interpreted cautiously, as year-to-year global averages may shift simply because high-violence or low-violence countries entered or exited the dataset in a given year.</li>
                <li><strong>Regional Variations in Definitions:</strong> What constitutes "intentional homicide" can vary slightly in legal definitions across jurisdictions.</li>
                <li><strong>UK Data:</strong> As noted in the source brief, UK data primarily refers to England and Wales.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="insights" style="background: #eef2f5; border-left-color: #3498db;">
            <strong>Policy Recommendations:</strong>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Targeted Interventions for Young Males:</strong> Given the staggering {m_f_ratio:.1f}x gender disparity and the peak in youth demographics, preventative resources must be aggressively targeted at young males in high-risk regions.</li>
                <li><strong>Invest in Data Capacity:</strong> The erratic reporting coverage highlights a need for international investment in robust, standardized crime reporting infrastructure, particularly in developing nations.</li>
                <li><strong>Focus on Hotspots:</strong> Global averages obscure the reality that homicide is intensely hyper-localized. International aid and security cooperation should focus on the distinct outliers driving the elevated global mean.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </section>

    <footer style="text-align: center; margin-top: 50px; padding-top: 20px; border-top: 1px solid #dee2e6; color: #777; font-size: 0.9em;">
        Generated for Public Safety Stakeholders & Policy Analysts | Data Source: UNODC Crime Trends Survey (via Kaggle)
    </footer>

</body>
</html>
"""

# Save to output
import os
os.makedirs('/app/output', exist_ok=True)
with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html_content)

print("Report generated at /app/output/final_report.html")
